  "title_lines": [
   "CATEGORIFIED ALGEBRA AND QUANTUM MECHANICS",
   "JEFFREY MORTON"
  ],
  "abstract": "Interest in combinatorial interpretations of mathematica l entities stems from the convenience of the concrete models they provi de. Finding a bijective proof of a seemingly obscure identity can reveal u nsuspected signi\ufb01- cance to it. Finding a combinatorial model for some mathemat ical entity is a particular instance of the process called \u201ccategori\ufb01catio n\u201d. Examples include the interpretation of /C6 as the Burnside rig of the category of \ufb01nite sets with product and coproduct, and the interpretation of /C6 [x] as the category of com- binatorial species. This has interesting applications to q uantum mechanics, and in particular the quantum harmonic oscillator, via Joya l\u2019s \u201cspecies\u201d, a new generalization called \u201cstu\ufb00 types\u201d, and operators betw een these, which can be represented as rudimentary Feynman diagrams for the o scillator. In quantum mechanics, we want to represent states in an algebra over the com- plex numbers, and also want our Feynman diagrams to carry mor e structure than these \u201cstu\ufb00 operators\u201d can do, and these turn out to be cl osely related. W e will show how to construct a combinatorial model for the qu antum har- monic oscillator in which the group of phases, U (1), plays a special role. W e describe a general notion of \u201c M -Stu\ufb00 Types\u201d for any monoid M , and see that the case M = U (1) provides an interpretation of time evolution in the comb i- natorial setting, as well as other quantum mechanical featu res of the harmonic oscillator. 1",
